    "findings": [
      {
        "text": "The fastest way to misjudge this category is to compare feature lists instead of behavior. Confirm processor support against the plan tier you'll actually pay for, test double-booking prevention with a real overlapping booking rather than trusting a claim, price the team you have rather than a single seat, and add the processor's own transaction fee before calling any number final. Tools like Cal.com pair Stripe payment acceptance with automatic schedule cross-referencing, and that combination of criteria, applied consistently, is what actually separates the options rather than a longer feature checklist.",
        "reason": "The four-step comparison conclusion (processor fit, double-booking test, team pricing, fees) is restated almost verbatim in passages 57, 60, and 63.",
        "removed": false,
        "category": "repetition",
        "severity": "editorial"
      },
      {
        "text": "the booking page collects a card (or PayPal balance) at the moment the appointment is confirmed",
        "reason": "No source describes generic timing mechanics of card/PayPal balance collection at confirmation moment.",
        "removed": false,
        "category": "qualitative",
        "severity": "material"
      },
      {
        "text": "a paid booking is a lot harder to no-show on than a free one",
        "reason": "No source quantifies or confirms this causal no-show claim in the assigned passage's general form.",
        "removed": false,
        "category": "qualitative",
        "severity": "material"
      },
      {
        "text": "the processor's transaction fee is separate from the software's subscription fee, and it usually isn't waived just because you're paying for a premium plan",
